is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router2
diagnosis_text:
The failure is in the routing control-plane on router2: FRRouting (bgpd) is down and has no BGP configuration. Mechanism: pc1’s traffic to 200.1.1.2 reaches router1, but router1 has no route to 200.1.1.0/24, so it returns ICMP “Destination Net Unreachable” (seen from pc1 pings). Inter-router L2/L3 is healthy (router1↔router2 ping succeeds), so the dataplane link is fine; the missing reachability is due to the BGP session not being established and routes not being exchanged.

Localization: 
- router1: FRR running; BGP neighbor 193.10.11.2 is stuck in Active (show ip bgp summary), and its routing table has only connected subnets.
- router2: systemd reports frr.service “Active: failed”; “bgpd is not running”; the FRR running config contains no “router bgp …” stanza, so even if FRR were up, no BGP peering would form.

Root cause: FRR/bgpd on router2 is not running and lacks BGP configuration, so no routes to 195.11.14.0/24 or 200.1.1.0/24 are exchanged.

Corrective fix: Do not restart router1. On router2, restore and restart the routing service: add BGP config (router bgp 2; neighbor 193.10.11.1 remote-as 1; address-family ipv4 unicast; network 200.1.1.0/24) and then systemctl restart frr. Verify “show ip bgp summary” is Established and both routers learn each other’s LAN prefixes. Connectivity between pc1 and pc2 will resume once BGP is up on router2.
=== END ===